window.ECOLOGY={"title": "Ecology: Unit 1 Practice", "description": "Seventh-grade practice based on the supplied Ecology Part 1 study guide.", "questions": [{"id": "eco-01", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "What does ecology study?", "options": ["How organisms interact with one another and their environment", "How rocks form deep inside Earth", "How only the organs inside one animal work", "How planets move around the Sun"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Ecology examines relationships among living things and between living things and their surroundings.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Ecology"}, {"id": "eco-02", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which set contains three biotic factors in a pond ecosystem?", "options": ["Sunlight, temperature, and water", "Frogs, sunlight, and rocks", "Algae, frogs, and bacteria", "Water, fish, and temperature"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Algae, frogs, and bacteria are living organisms. Biotic factors are the living parts of an ecosystem and material from once-living things.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Biotic Factor; three biotic examples"}, {"id": "eco-03", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which set contains three abiotic factors in a forest?", "options": ["Trees, fungi, and deer", "Sunlight, rainfall, and temperature", "Rainfall, trees, and squirrels", "Bacteria, sunlight, and mushrooms"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Abiotic factors are nonliving physical or chemical parts of the environment. Light, water availability, and temperature affect forest life.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Abiotic Factor; three abiotic examples"}, {"id": "eco-04", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "All the white-tailed deer living in one forest form a…", "options": ["community", "biome", "biosphere", "population"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A population consists of members of one species living in the same area at the same time.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Population"}, {"id": "eco-05", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "For many sexually reproducing organisms, which description best identifies a species?", "options": ["A group whose members can generally mate with one another and produce fertile offspring", "Every organism that lives in one forest", "All animals that eat the same kind of food", "A group with exactly the same size and color"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "This useful school-level definition focuses on the ability to produce offspring that can also reproduce. Looking alike or sharing a habitat is not enough.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Species"}, {"id": "eco-06", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "The deer, oak trees, foxes, fungi, and other living populations in a forest make up its…", "options": ["population", "abiotic factors", "community", "organism"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A community includes all the populations of different species in an area. It does not include nonliving factors.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Community"}, {"id": "eco-07", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which description includes an entire ecosystem?", "options": ["Only the fish of one species in a pond", "Pond organisms interacting with the water, sunlight, mud, and other surroundings", "Only the living populations in a pond", "Only the temperature and water in a pond"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An ecosystem includes a community and its nonliving environment, along with their interactions.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Ecosystem"}, {"id": "eco-08", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "A large region with a characteristic climate and typical plants and animals is a…", "options": ["population", "food chain", "species", "biome"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A desert, tropical rainforest, or tundra is a biome. A biome contains many ecosystems with broadly similar conditions.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Biome"}, {"id": "eco-09", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "What is the biosphere?", "options": ["All parts of Earth where life exists", "Only the oceans on Earth", "One community and its surroundings", "A single region with a similar climate"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The biosphere includes life in parts of the land, water, and atmosphere. It is the broadest level in this review.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Biosphere"}, {"id": "eco-10", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which sequence lists the six ecological levels from smallest to largest?", "options": ["Organism → community → population → ecosystem → biome → biosphere", "Population → organism → ecosystem → community → biosphere → biome", "Organism → population → community → ecosystem → biome → biosphere", "Organism → population → biome → community → ecosystem → biosphere"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Start with one living thing, then one species in an area, all living populations, their environment, a major climate region, and all life on Earth.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six levels of ecological organization"}, {"id": "eco-11", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which is one organism?", "options": ["All maple trees in a park", "One maple tree", "Every species living in a park", "The park and all its surroundings"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An organism is one individual living thing. One tree is an organism even though it contains many cells.", "guideRef": "Prompt: ecological organization; supplemental Organism"}, {"id": "eco-12", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which statement about cells is true of living organisms?", "options": ["Every organism has many cells", "Only animals are made of cells", "Cells are found only in organisms that move from place to place", "An organism is made of one or more cells"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Some organisms have one cell and others have many. Cells are the basic units of living organisms.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—cells"}, {"id": "eco-13", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which substance carries inherited instructions in living organisms?", "options": ["DNA", "Sunlight", "Water vapor", "Oxygen gas"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "DNA stores hereditary information. Organisms pass genetic information to their offspring through reproduction.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—heredity/DNA"}, {"id": "eco-14", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "A tadpole develops legs as it becomes a frog. Which life characteristic does this best show?", "options": ["Producing offspring", "Responding to a sudden touch", "Growth and development", "Keeping body temperature within a suitable range"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Growth is an increase in size or cell number; development includes changes in form and function as an organism matures.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—growth and development"}, {"id": "eco-15", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which statement correctly describes energy use by living things?", "options": ["Plants do not need energy because they stay in one place", "Both plants and animals use energy to carry out life processes", "Only growing organisms need energy", "Animals create energy from nothing"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "All organisms need energy for activities such as building cells, transport, and repair. Plants also use the energy stored in sugars.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—energy use"}, {"id": "eco-16", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "A seedling bends toward a window. Which life characteristic does this show?", "options": ["Changing species", "Making nitrogen gas", "Precipitating", "Responding to a stimulus"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Light is an environmental stimulus. The seedling changes its growth direction in response to it.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—response"}, {"id": "eco-17", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "Which statement best explains reproduction as a characteristic of life?", "options": ["Reproduction allows a kind of organism to continue, even though some individuals never reproduce", "Every living individual must produce offspring to count as alive", "Reproduction means that an individual becomes larger", "Only animals reproduce; plants cannot"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Reproduction produces new organisms and continues a species or lineage. A living individual does not have to reproduce during its lifetime.", "guideRef": "Prompt: six characteristics of living things—reproduction"}, {"id": "eco-18", "topic": "foundations", "prompt": "A runner sweats as body temperature rises. How does this help homeostasis?", "options": ["Sweat makes internal temperature identical to outdoor temperature at all times", "Sweat stops every chemical reaction in the body", "Evaporating sweat helps keep internal temperature within a suitable range", "Sweat makes body temperature rise without limit"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Homeostasis keeps internal conditions relatively stable through adjustments. It does not mean that conditions never change.", "guideRef": "Prompt: describe homeostasis"}, {"id": "eco-19", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Why is a grass plant a producer?", "options": ["It gets all its food by eating insects", "It makes sugars from carbon dioxide and water using light energy", "It absorbs ready-made sugar as its main food from soil", "It breaks down dead animals for all its energy"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A producer makes organic food from simpler substances. Most producers in familiar land ecosystems use photosynthesis.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Producer; producers versus consumers"}, {"id": "eco-20", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "What is the main difference between producers and consumers?", "options": ["Producers use energy; consumers do not", "Consumers make all their food from sunlight; producers eat it", "Producers are living; consumers are nonliving", "Producers make their own organic food; consumers obtain food from other organisms or their remains"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Both need energy. They differ in how they obtain energy-rich organic food.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Consumer; producers versus consumers"}, {"id": "eco-21", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Which process allows grass to store sunlight energy in food?", "options": ["Photosynthesis", "Condensation", "Predation", "Nitrogen fixation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "During photosynthesis, light energy helps build sugars. The plant can use the chemical energy stored in those sugars.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Photosynthesis; energy source"}, {"id": "eco-22", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "What is the ultimate energy source for the forest food chains in this study guide?", "options": ["The foxes at the top", "The minerals in rocks", "The Sun", "The decomposers alone"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Sunlight supplies energy that plants capture. That stored energy passes through feeding relationships. Some unusual ecosystems use chemical energy instead.", "guideRef": "Prompt: ultimate energy source of a food chain/web"}, {"id": "eco-23", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Which pair includes two common decomposers?", "options": ["Hawks and foxes", "Many fungi and bacteria", "Oak trees and grass", "Rabbits and deer"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Many fungi and bacteria chemically break down dead material and wastes. Their work releases nutrients that other organisms can use.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Decomposer; two decomposer examples"}, {"id": "eco-24", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "What is a major role of decomposers?", "options": ["Creating new sunlight for producers", "Stopping matter from cycling through ecosystems", "Changing all consumers into producers", "Breaking down remains and wastes and returning nutrients to the environment"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Decomposers recycle matter. They do not recycle lost heat back into sunlight or create new energy.", "guideRef": "Prompt: what decomposers do"}, {"id": "eco-25", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "A rabbit eats grasses and leaves. Which consumer label fits this diet?", "options": ["Herbivore", "Carnivore", "Scavenger", "Producer"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A herbivore eats plants or other producers. The rabbit still gets its food by consuming other organisms.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Herbivore"}, {"id": "eco-26", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "An animal that gets its food by eating other animals is a…", "options": ["producer", "herbivore", "carnivore", "biome"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Carnivores eat animals. A hawk that eats mice is one example.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Carnivore"}, {"id": "eco-27", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "A raccoon eats berries, insects, and small fish. It is a…", "options": ["herbivore", "omnivore", "producer", "nitrogen fixer"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An omnivore eats both producers, such as plants, and animals. Its feeding level can depend on the meal.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Omnivore"}, {"id": "eco-28", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "A vulture eats the remains of an animal it did not kill. In this example, it acts as a…", "options": ["producer", "herbivore", "plant parasite", "scavenger"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Scavengers consume dead animals. Decomposers such as fungi and bacteria break material down chemically; a vulture is a scavenging consumer.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Scavenger"}, {"id": "eco-29", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "How does a food web differ from a food chain?", "options": ["A food web connects multiple feeding paths; a food chain shows one path", "A food web contains only plants; a chain contains only animals", "A food chain shows every feeding relationship in an ecosystem", "A food web shows energy moving only from predators to their prey"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A food chain follows a single route of feeding and energy transfer. A food web combines interconnected chains.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Food Chain and Food Web; compare chains/webs"}, {"id": "eco-30", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "What is the first organism in a complete, producer-based food chain?", "options": ["A tertiary consumer", "A predator", "A producer", "A scavenger"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The producer forms the first feeding level. The Sun is an energy source, but it is not an organism.", "guideRef": "Prompt: first organism in a food chain"}, {"id": "eco-31", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "In the chain grass → rabbit → fox, what does the arrow from rabbit to fox show?", "options": ["The rabbit hunts the fox", "Energy and matter move from the rabbit to the fox when the fox eats the rabbit", "The fox gives sunlight to the rabbit", "The rabbit and fox are the same species"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Food arrows point from the food to the eater. The arrow follows the direction of energy transfer.", "guideRef": "Prompt: meaning and direction of food arrows"}, {"id": "eco-32", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "In grass → grasshopper → mouse → hawk, the grasshopper eats only the grass in this chain. What is its level?", "options": ["Producer", "Secondary consumer", "Tertiary consumer", "Primary consumer"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A primary consumer eats a producer. Grass is the producer at the base of this chain.", "guideRef": "Prompt: label a chain with three consumers"}, {"id": "eco-33", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "In grass → grasshopper → mouse → hawk, which labels correctly describe the last two organisms?", "options": ["Mouse: secondary consumer; hawk: tertiary consumer", "Mouse: primary consumer; hawk: secondary consumer", "Mouse: producer; hawk: primary consumer", "Mouse: tertiary consumer; hawk: producer"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The mouse eats the primary consumer, and the hawk eats that secondary consumer. These labels describe their positions in this particular chain.", "guideRef": "Prompt: secondary and tertiary consumers"}, {"id": "eco-34", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "The forest picture includes grass, a rabbit, and a fox. If the rabbit eats grass and the fox eats the rabbit, which chain is correct?", "options": ["Fox → rabbit → grass", "Rabbit → grass → fox", "Grass → rabbit → fox", "Grass → fox → rabbit"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Each arrow points from food to eater. These organisms appear in the picture; the stated feeding facts supply the links.", "guideRef": "Prompt: create a chain from the forest food-web picture"}, {"id": "eco-35", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Suppose a grasshopper eats grass, a mouse eats the grasshopper, and a hawk eats the mouse. Which chain contains one producer and exactly three consumers?", "options": ["Grass → grasshopper → mouse", "Grass → grasshopper → mouse → hawk", "Hawk → mouse → grasshopper → grass", "Grasshopper → grass → mouse → hawk"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Grass is the producer. Grasshopper, mouse, and hawk are the primary, secondary, and tertiary consumers, respectively.", "guideRef": "Prompt: draw a food chain with three consumers and arrows"}, {"id": "eco-36", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Using the usual classroom approximation, about what fraction of energy at one feeding level becomes available at the next?", "options": ["90%", "50%", "100%", "10%"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The 10% rule is an approximate model of energy transfer between feeding levels. Actual transfer efficiency varies among ecosystems.", "guideRef": "Prompt: how much energy passes to the next level"}, {"id": "eco-37", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "A food chain starts with 10,000 energy units stored at the producer level. Using the 10% rule, about how many reach the secondary consumer level?", "options": ["100 energy units", "1,000 energy units", "9,000 energy units", "10 energy units"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Two transfers occur: 10,000 × 0.10 = 1,000 at primary consumers, then 1,000 × 0.10 = 100 at secondary consumers.", "guideRef": "Prompt: energy transfer; Energy pyramid"}, {"id": "eco-38", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "Why does much of the energy at one feeding level fail to reach the next consumer level?", "options": ["The energy vanishes from the universe", "All energy is returned to the Sun", "Organisms use energy in life processes and release heat; some energy stays in uneaten material or wastes", "Consumers convert all energy directly into minerals"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Energy is transformed, not destroyed. Heat is less available to the food chain, and uneaten material or wastes can enter decomposer pathways.", "guideRef": "Prompt: what happens to the remaining energy"}, {"id": "eco-39", "topic": "energy", "prompt": "In an energy pyramid, which feeding level usually has the greatest amount of energy available?", "options": ["The tertiary consumer level at the top", "The producer level at the base", "Every level has the same amount", "Only the level with the largest individual animals"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An energy pyramid narrows upward because less energy is available at each successive feeding level.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Energy pyramid"}, {"id": "eco-40", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "An owl catches and eats a mouse. Which labels are correct?", "options": ["The mouse is the predator, and the owl is the prey", "Both are producers", "Both are decomposers", "The owl is the predator, and the mouse is the prey"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A predator catches and eats another organism. The organism it eats is the prey in that interaction.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Predator and Prey; predator–prey relationships"}, {"id": "eco-41", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "In a simple system, rabbits are a fox population’s main food. Rabbit numbers rise while other conditions stay similar. What might happen next?", "options": ["Fox numbers may rise after a delay because more food is available", "Fox numbers must fall immediately", "The foxes will become producers", "Rabbit and fox numbers must stay exactly equal"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "More prey can support more predators, but survival and reproduction take time. Real populations also depend on other conditions.", "guideRef": "Prompt: predator–prey relationships—population trends"}, {"id": "eco-42", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "If a predator population increases and other conditions stay similar, what may happen to its prey population?", "options": ["The prey population must increase without limit", "The prey stop needing food", "The prey population may decrease because more prey are eaten", "Predation can no longer affect prey numbers"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Predators can reduce prey numbers. If prey later become scarce, fewer predators may survive or reproduce.", "guideRef": "Prompt: predator–prey relationships—population trends"}, {"id": "eco-43", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "For a single predation event, how are the predator and the prey affected?", "options": ["Both benefit (+/+)", "The predator benefits (+), and the prey is harmed (−)", "Both are unaffected (0/0)", "The prey benefits (+), and the predator is harmed (−)"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The predator gains food while the prey is harmed. This describes the individuals in the feeding event, not every effect on the ecosystem.", "guideRef": "Prompt: predator–prey relationships—predation"}, {"id": "eco-44", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "Two bird species need the same limited nesting holes. What interaction occurs?", "options": ["Condensation", "Mutualism", "Photosynthesis", "Competition"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Competition happens when organisms use the same limited resource. Here the resource is nesting space.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Competition"}, {"id": "eco-45", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "Which statement about competition is correct?", "options": ["It can occur within one species or between different species", "It occurs only between predators and prey", "It occurs only between different species", "It occurs only when resources are unlimited"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Two deer can compete for food, and deer and rabbits can also compete for food. Limited shared resources are the key.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Competition; relationship review"}, {"id": "eco-46", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "Members of a wolf pack work together to catch prey. Their teamwork is an example of…", "options": ["competition among the pack members in this example", "commensalism between the wolves and prey", "cooperation", "nitrogen fixation"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Cooperation means organisms work together in ways that benefit the participants. The wolves’ interaction with their prey is still predation.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Cooperation; relationship review"}, {"id": "eco-47", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "During a drought, a shrinking water supply slows a deer population’s growth. Water is acting as a…", "options": ["producer", "limiting factor", "symbiosis", "food web"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A limiting factor restricts population size or growth. Food, space, water, disease, and predators can all limit populations.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Limiting Factor"}, {"id": "eco-48", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "What does carrying capacity describe?", "options": ["The greatest speed at which an animal can run", "The number of different biomes on Earth", "The exact number of births in one day", "The population size an environment can support over time with its available resources"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Carrying capacity depends on resources and conditions. A population can temporarily exceed it, but shortages can then reduce the population.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Carrying Capacity"}, {"id": "eco-49", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "A forest loses much of the vegetation deer eat. If no replacement food appears, what will likely happen to its carrying capacity for deer?", "options": ["It will decrease", "It will increase automatically", "It cannot change because carrying capacity is always fixed", "It will become the number of predators only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Less available food generally means the forest can support fewer deer over time.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Carrying Capacity and Limiting Factor"}, {"id": "eco-50", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "What is symbiosis?", "options": ["Any brief encounter between two animals", "Competition only among members of one species", "A close, ongoing relationship between organisms of different species", "The movement of water through an ecosystem"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "This review uses symbiosis for close, ongoing interactions between different species, including mutualism, commensalism, and parasitism.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Symbiosis"}, {"id": "eco-51", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "Fungi help plant roots absorb nutrients, and the plant supplies sugars to the fungi. Both benefit. This is…", "options": ["parasitism", "mutualism", "commensalism", "competition"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Mutualism is a close relationship in which both species benefit: +/+.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Mutualism; symbiosis chart"}, {"id": "eco-52", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "An orchid grows on a tree to reach light. In this example, the orchid benefits and the tree is neither helped nor harmed. This is…", "options": ["mutualism", "parasitism", "predation", "commensalism"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Commensalism is +/0: one species benefits and the other has no meaningful benefit or harm in the stated example.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Commensalism; symbiosis chart"}, {"id": "eco-53", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "A tick obtains food from a dog’s blood and harms the dog. This relationship is…", "options": ["parasitism", "mutualism", "commensalism", "cooperation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "In parasitism, the parasite benefits and its host is harmed: +/−. The parasite often lives on or inside its host.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Parasitism; symbiosis chart"}, {"id": "eco-54", "topic": "relationships", "prompt": "Using + for benefit, − for harm, and 0 for no effect, which symbiosis chart is correct?", "options": ["Mutualism +/−; commensalism +/+; parasitism +/0", "Mutualism 0/0; commensalism −/−; parasitism +/+", "Mutualism +/+; commensalism +/0; parasitism +/−", "Mutualism +/0; commensalism +/−; parasitism 0/0"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Both benefit in mutualism, only one benefits in commensalism, and the parasite benefits while the host is harmed in parasitism. Reversing species order reverses the signs’ order.", "guideRef": "Prompt: complete the symbiosis chart"}, {"id": "eco-55", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "What does the water cycle describe?", "options": ["Only liquid water moving, with no changes of state", "Water moving among the atmosphere, land, organisms, and bodies of water", "Only rainfall, with no return of water to the atmosphere", "Water moving from rivers to oceans and never returning"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Water is stored and moved through many connected paths. It can change among solid, liquid, and gas.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Water cycle"}, {"id": "eco-56", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "A puddle gets smaller on a dry day without draining into the ground. Water becomes a gas. Which process is occurring?", "options": ["Condensation", "Precipitation", "Nitrogen fixation", "Evaporation"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Evaporation changes liquid water into water vapor at the surface. It can occur well below the boiling point.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Evaporation; how evaporation occurs"}, {"id": "eco-57", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "What supplies most of the energy that drives evaporation in Earth’s water cycle?", "options": ["The Sun warming water and its surroundings", "Earth’s internal heat warming all surface water", "Gravity alone pulling water out of lakes", "Chemical energy released by aquatic animals"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Energy allows water molecules to escape liquid water. Solar energy is the main driver of evaporation on Earth.", "guideRef": "Prompt: what causes evaporation"}, {"id": "eco-58", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Water vapor in cooling air forms tiny liquid droplets that make a cloud. This change is…", "options": ["evaporation", "transpiration", "condensation", "photosynthesis"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Condensation changes water vapor into liquid water. Cloud droplets are liquid; water vapor itself is an invisible gas.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Condensation"}, {"id": "eco-59", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which event is precipitation?", "options": ["A puddle changing into water vapor", "Snow falling from clouds to the ground", "Water vapor leaving a leaf", "Water droplets forming on a cold glass"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Precipitation is water falling from the atmosphere, including rain, snow, sleet, and hail.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Precipitation"}, {"id": "eco-60", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "A plant takes up water through its roots, and water vapor leaves its leaves. The release from the leaves is called…", "options": ["nitrogen fixation", "precipitation", "predation", "transpiration"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Transpiration moves water from plants into the atmosphere as vapor.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Transpiration"}, {"id": "eco-61", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Lake water becomes vapor, cloud droplets form, and then rain falls. Which process sequence matches that path?", "options": ["Evaporation → condensation → precipitation", "Condensation → evaporation → precipitation", "Precipitation → transpiration → condensation", "Evaporation → precipitation → condensation"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Liquid water can evaporate, water vapor can condense into droplets, and those droplets can fall as precipitation. The cycle has many paths and no required starting point.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Water cycle, Evaporation, Condensation, Precipitation"}, {"id": "eco-62", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which example traces carbon moving through living and nonliving parts of an ecosystem?", "options": ["Lake water evaporates, condenses into clouds, and falls as rain", "Bacteria fix nitrogen gas, and plants take up nitrogen compounds", "Air’s carbon dioxide enters a plant, a rabbit eats the plant, and respiration returns carbon dioxide to the air", "Oxygen dissolves in lake water, and fish take it in through their gills"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The carbon cycle moves carbon among organisms and environmental stores such as air, water, and soil.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Carbon cycle"}, {"id": "eco-63", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "What does photosynthesis do in the carbon cycle?", "options": ["Changes carbon atoms into nitrogen atoms", "Moves carbon from carbon dioxide into sugars and other plant materials", "Removes all carbon from plants", "Uses only oxygen to build sugars"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Plants take in carbon dioxide, and its carbon becomes part of food molecules. That carbon can later pass to consumers.", "guideRef": "Prompt: role of photosynthesis in the carbon cycle"}, {"id": "eco-64", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "How do animals contribute to the carbon cycle?", "options": ["They get all their carbon directly from sunlight", "They remove carbon from the cycle forever", "They perform nitrogen fixation instead of eating", "They gain carbon in food and release some as carbon dioxide through cellular respiration"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Animals also pass carbon into wastes and remains. Decomposers can return some of that carbon to the environment.", "guideRef": "Prompt: how animals contribute to the carbon cycle"}, {"id": "eco-65", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which summary describes photosynthesis in a green plant?", "options": ["Carbon dioxide + water + light energy → sugars + oxygen", "Sugars + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + released energy", "Carbon dioxide + oxygen + light energy → water only", "Water vapor → liquid water droplets"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Photosynthesis uses light energy to build sugars from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen. The option that breaks down sugar using oxygen describes aerobic cellular respiration.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Photosynthesis; carbon and oxygen cycles"}, {"id": "eco-66", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which pair of processes is central to this unit’s oxygen cycle?", "options": ["Photosynthesis uses oxygen, and aerobic respiration releases oxygen", "Both photosynthesis and aerobic respiration only release oxygen", "Photosynthesis releases oxygen, and aerobic respiration uses oxygen", "Only animals produce oxygen, and only plants consume it"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Oxygen moves through the environment and organisms. Photosynthesis and aerobic cellular respiration connect the oxygen and carbon cycles.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Oxygen cycle"}, {"id": "eco-67", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "How do photosynthesizing plants and animals connect the oxygen and carbon cycles?", "options": ["Plants release only nitrogen gas; animals use it to make sugars", "Plants release oxygen that animals can use; animals release carbon dioxide that plants can use", "Plants and animals both make their food directly from carbon dioxide and sunlight", "Animals release only oxygen, and plants release only carbon dioxide"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Photosynthesis supplies oxygen, while animal respiration returns carbon dioxide. Plants also carry out respiration and can use carbon dioxide from other sources.", "guideRef": "Prompt: how plants and animals depend on each other in the oxygen cycle"}, {"id": "eco-68", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "What happens during aerobic cellular respiration?", "options": ["Cells use sunlight to make sugar from carbon dioxide", "Liquid water becomes water vapor on a leaf", "Bacteria turn nitrogen gas into ammonia", "Cells use oxygen to release usable energy from food, producing carbon dioxide and water"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Cellular respiration releases energy stored in food. Breathing exchanges gases with the environment; it helps supply oxygen but is not the cell’s chemical process itself.", "guideRef": "Prompt: respiration and the oxygen cycle; supplemental Respiration"}, {"id": "eco-69", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which statement about plant cells is correct?", "options": ["Plant cells carry out cellular respiration to obtain usable energy from food", "Plant cells never use oxygen", "Plants carry out respiration only when animals are nearby", "Plants stop all energy use after sunset"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Plants use cellular respiration as well as photosynthesis. Respiration continues day and night; photosynthesis requires light.", "guideRef": "Prompt: respiration and the oxygen cycle"}, {"id": "eco-70", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Which statement about the nitrogen cycle is correct?", "options": ["Most plants absorb nitrogen gas directly through their leaves and use it unchanged", "Animals get all their nitrogen by breathing nitrogen gas", "Nitrogen moves among the atmosphere, soil, water, and organisms in different forms", "Nitrogen moves from the air into soil but never returns to the air"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Most plants take up usable nitrogen compounds through roots, and animals obtain nitrogen by eating. Nitrogen is needed for proteins and DNA.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Nitrogen cycle"}, {"id": "eco-71", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "What is biological nitrogen fixation?", "options": ["Plant roots absorb nitrate already present in the soil", "Certain microbes convert nitrogen gas into ammonia or related usable nitrogen compounds", "Bacteria convert nitrate into nitrogen gas that returns to the air", "Animals obtain nitrogen compounds by eating food"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Nitrogen-fixing microbes include bacteria in soil and in some plant roots. They make atmospheric nitrogen available to living systems.", "guideRef": "Vocabulary: Nitrogen fixation; bacteria in the nitrogen cycle"}, {"id": "eco-72", "topic": "cycles", "prompt": "Besides nitrogen fixation, how can different bacteria help the nitrogen cycle?", "options": ["All bacteria perform exactly one identical nitrogen reaction", "They store nitrogen forever and never release it", "They return nitrogen to the atmosphere only by photosynthesis", "They recycle nitrogen from wastes and remains, transform soil nitrogen compounds, or return nitrogen gas to the air"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Different bacterial groups do different jobs. Together they help move nitrogen into usable forms and return it to environmental stores.", "guideRef": "Prompt: role of bacteria in the nitrogen cycle"}], "glossary": [{"term": "Ecology", "definition": "The study of interactions among organisms and between organisms and their environment.", "example": "Studying how rainfall affects grass and grazing animals."}, {"term": "Biotic Factor", "definition": "A living part of an ecosystem, or material that comes from a once-living organism.", "example": "Oak trees, deer, and bacteria are biotic factors."}, {"term": "Abiotic Factor", "definition": "A nonliving physical or chemical part of the environment.", "example": "Sunlight, temperature, and rainfall are abiotic factors."}, {"term": "Community", "definition": "All the populations of different species living in one area.", "example": "All the fish, algae, insects, and microbes in a pond."}, {"term": "Ecosystem", "definition": "A community, its nonliving surroundings, and their interactions.", "example": "A pond’s organisms interacting with water, light, and nutrients."}, {"term": "Biome", "definition": "A large region with a characteristic climate and typical organisms.", "example": "Tundra, desert, and tropical rainforest."}, {"term": "Biosphere", "definition": "All parts of Earth where life exists; the global collection of ecosystems.", "example": "Life in oceans, on land, and in parts of the atmosphere."}, {"term": "Population", "definition": "Members of one species living in the same area at the same time.", "example": "All the white-tailed deer in one forest this year."}, {"term": "Species", "definition": "For many sexually reproducing organisms, a group that can generally mate and produce fertile offspring.", "example": "White-tailed deer form one species; sharing a forest does not make deer and foxes one species."}, {"term": "Producer", "definition": "An organism that makes organic food from simpler substances using light or chemical energy.", "example": "Grass uses sunlight to make sugars."}, {"term": "Decomposer", "definition": "An organism that chemically breaks down dead material or wastes and releases nutrients.", "example": "Many bacteria and fungi decompose fallen leaves."}, {"term": "Consumer", "definition": "An organism that obtains organic food from other organisms or their remains.", "example": "A rabbit eats grass; a fox eats a rabbit."}, {"term": "Herbivore", "definition": "A consumer that eats plants or other producers.", "example": "A deer grazing on plants."}, {"term": "Carnivore", "definition": "A consumer that eats other animals.", "example": "A hawk eating a mouse."}, {"term": "Omnivore", "definition": "A consumer that eats both producers and animals.", "example": "A raccoon eating berries and fish."}, {"term": "Scavenger", "definition": "A consumer that eats dead animals, often ones it did not kill.", "example": "A vulture eating a carcass."}, {"term": "Food Chain", "definition": "A model showing one pathway of feeding and energy transfer.", "example": "Grass → rabbit → fox."}, {"term": "Food Web", "definition": "A model connecting multiple food chains in an ecosystem.", "example": "Grass feeds rabbits and mice, and both can feed foxes."}, {"term": "Energy pyramid", "definition": "A model showing decreasing available energy at successively higher feeding levels.", "example": "Producers at the broad base support fewer energy units at the top."}, {"term": "Carrying Capacity", "definition": "The population size an environment can support over time under its current conditions.", "example": "A habitat with less food can support fewer deer."}, {"term": "Limiting Factor", "definition": "A resource or environmental condition that restricts population size or growth.", "example": "Drought limits plant growth by reducing available water."}, {"term": "Cooperation", "definition": "Organisms working together in ways that benefit the participants.", "example": "Wolves working together during a hunt."}, {"term": "Carbon cycle", "definition": "The movement of carbon among organisms, air, water, soil, and other Earth stores.", "example": "Plants take in carbon dioxide; respiration returns it."}, {"term": "Nitrogen cycle", "definition": "The movement and transformation of nitrogen among organisms and the environment.", "example": "Plants absorb nitrogen compounds and animals gain nitrogen by eating."}, {"term": "Water cycle", "definition": "The continuous movement of water through Earth’s surface, atmosphere, underground, and organisms.", "example": "Water evaporates, condenses, and returns as precipitation."}, {"term": "Oxygen cycle", "definition": "The movement of oxygen through living things and the environment; photosynthesis and respiration are major biological parts.", "example": "A plant releases oxygen during photosynthesis, and an animal uses oxygen in aerobic respiration."}, {"term": "Photosynthesis", "definition": "In plants, the use of light energy to make sugars from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen.", "example": "A sunlit leaf makes sugars."}, {"term": "Evaporation", "definition": "The change of liquid water into water vapor at its surface.", "example": "A puddle slowly dries on a warm day."}, {"term": "Condensation", "definition": "The change of water vapor into liquid water.", "example": "Droplets form on the outside of a cold glass."}, {"term": "Precipitation", "definition": "Water that falls from clouds to Earth as liquid or solid.", "example": "Rain, snow, sleet, and hail."}, {"term": "Transpiration", "definition": "The release of water vapor from plants, mainly through leaves.", "example": "Water taken up by roots later escapes from leaf openings as vapor."}, {"term": "Nitrogen fixation", "definition": "The conversion of nitrogen gas into usable nitrogen compounds; certain microbes perform biological fixation.", "example": "Bacteria in some bean-family root nodules convert nitrogen gas into ammonia."}, {"term": "Predator", "definition": "An organism that catches and eats another organism in a feeding interaction.", "example": "An owl catching a mouse."}, {"term": "Prey", "definition": "An organism that a predator catches and eats.", "example": "A mouse eaten by an owl."}, {"term": "Symbiosis", "definition": "A close, ongoing relationship between organisms of different species.", "example": "A root-associated fungus and a plant living in close association."}, {"term": "Mutualism", "definition": "A symbiotic relationship in which both species benefit: +/+.", "example": "A plant gives sugars to root fungi, and the fungi help the plant obtain nutrients."}, {"term": "Commensalism", "definition": "A symbiotic relationship in which one species benefits and the other is not meaningfully helped or harmed: +/0.", "example": "An orchid gains a growing place on a tree without affecting the tree in the stated example."}, {"term": "Parasitism", "definition": "A symbiotic relationship in which a parasite benefits and its host is harmed: +/−.", "example": "A tick feeds on a dog’s blood."}, {"term": "Competition", "definition": "An interaction in which organisms use the same limited resource.", "example": "Two bird species compete for limited nesting holes."}, {"term": "Organism", "definition": "One individual living thing.", "example": "One frog, one tree, or one bacterial cell."}, {"term": "Homeostasis", "definition": "The regulation of internal conditions so they stay within suitable ranges.", "example": "Sweating helps a warm human body cool down."}, {"term": "Respiration", "definition": "Cellular respiration releases usable energy from food; aerobic respiration uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water.", "example": "Plant and animal cells use aerobic respiration, even when the organism is resting."}], "notes": [{"topic": "foundations", "title": "Six levels, one expanding view", "body": "Organism → population → community → ecosystem → biome → biosphere. Picture one deer, all deer of that species in a forest, all living populations there, those populations plus the nonliving environment, a major climate region, and finally all life on Earth."}, {"topic": "foundations", "title": "Living and nonliving factors", "body": "Biotic examples: trees, fungi, deer. Abiotic examples: sunlight, rainfall, temperature. A community includes living populations; an ecosystem includes those populations and their abiotic environment. Soil contains both nonliving minerals and living organisms, so name the specific part when classifying it."}, {"topic": "foundations", "title": "Six characteristics of life", "body": "This review uses six groups: (1) made of cells, (2) hereditary information in DNA, (3) growth and development, (4) energy use, (5) response to the environment and regulation of internal conditions, and (6) reproduction. Classrooms can group these ideas differently. Reproduction continues a species or lineage; some living individuals never reproduce."}, {"topic": "foundations", "title": "Homeostasis means a suitable range", "body": "Homeostasis uses adjustments to keep internal conditions relatively stable. Sweating cools the body; regulating water balance protects cells. Stable does not mean completely unchanging, and responding to a stimulus does not always involve movement from place to place."}, {"topic": "energy", "title": "Food roles", "body": "Producers make organic food; consumers obtain it from organisms or their remains. Herbivores eat producers, carnivores eat animals, and omnivores eat both. Scavengers consume dead animals. Many fungi and bacteria are decomposers that chemically break down remains and recycle nutrients."}, {"topic": "energy", "title": "Follow the arrow", "body": "A food chain follows one feeding path; a food web joins many paths. Arrows point from food to eater because energy and matter pass that way. Sunlight powers the producer-based forest web, but the Sun is not an organism. A complete chain in this model starts with a producer."}, {"topic": "energy", "title": "A four-level practice chain", "body": "Suppose grasshoppers eat grass, mice eat the grasshoppers, and hawks eat the mice: grass (producer) → grasshopper (primary consumer) → mouse (secondary consumer) → hawk (tertiary consumer). There are three consumers. An omnivore’s consumer level depends on the particular food path."}, {"topic": "energy", "title": "Use the forest picture carefully", "body": "The supplied forest picture shows organisms but leaves feeding arrows for the student to draw. A valid example with stated feeding links is grass → rabbit → fox. To practice a chain with three consumers, use grass → grasshopper → mouse → hawk with the feeding assumptions stated above; do not treat undrawn links as printed evidence."}, {"topic": "energy", "title": "Energy gets less available up the pyramid", "body": "Use about 10% transfer per feeding level for classroom calculations: 10,000 producer units → 1,000 primary-consumer units → 100 secondary-consumer units → 10 tertiary-consumer units. Organisms use energy and release heat, while uneaten material and wastes can enter decomposer pathways. Energy flows; matter cycles. Actual transfer percentages vary."}, {"topic": "relationships", "title": "Predators and prey affect each other", "body": "A predator benefits by eating prey, which is harmed in that event. More prey may support more predators after a delay; more predators may then reduce prey. When prey become scarce, predator survival or reproduction may fall. These are possible patterns, not exact predictions for every ecosystem."}, {"topic": "relationships", "title": "Review the relationship ideas separately", "body": "The phrase “two types of relationships between predators and prey” is not a standard two-part classification. Learn predation and predator–prey population patterns, plus competition and cooperation. Competition involves a shared limited resource, within or between species. Cooperation involves organisms working together for mutual benefit."}, {"topic": "relationships", "title": "Resources set population limits", "body": "Limiting factors restrict growth: water, food, space, disease, and predators are examples. Carrying capacity is the population size an environment can sustain over time. It can change when resources or conditions change, and populations may temporarily rise above it."}, {"topic": "relationships", "title": "Symbiosis chart", "body": "+ means benefits; − means harmed; 0 means neither helped nor harmed. Mutualism: +/+. Commensalism: +/0. Parasitism: +/−, with the parasite benefiting and host harmed. If species columns are reversed, 0/+ and −/+ describe the same respective relationship. Symbiosis involves close, ongoing association between different species."}, {"topic": "cycles", "title": "Water: change state, change place", "body": "Solar energy drives much evaporation: liquid → vapor. Condensation is vapor → liquid droplets. Precipitation is rain, snow, sleet, or hail falling from clouds. Transpiration releases water vapor from leaves. Water can also soak into soil, flow as runoff, or collect in rivers and oceans. A cycle has no required beginning."}, {"topic": "cycles", "title": "Carbon follows food and respiration", "body": "Photosynthesis takes carbon dioxide from the surroundings and builds carbon into sugars. Animals gain carbon by eating and release some as carbon dioxide during cellular respiration. Waste and dead organisms pass carbon to decomposers. Carbon also moves through water, soil, and rocks."}, {"topic": "cycles", "title": "Photosynthesis and oxygen", "body": "For green plants: carbon dioxide + water + light energy → sugars + oxygen. Photosynthesis adds oxygen to the surroundings and stores light energy in food. Plants and animals can use oxygen in aerobic cellular respiration. Plants also need carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, including carbon dioxide returned by respiration."}, {"topic": "cycles", "title": "Respiration happens in cells", "body": "Aerobic cellular respiration: sugars + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + usable energy. Breathing moves gases; respiration is the chemical process in cells. Plants carry out respiration day and night. Photosynthesis requires light, so a plant can respire when it is not photosynthesizing."}, {"topic": "cycles", "title": "Nitrogen needs transformations", "body": "Most plants cannot use atmospheric nitrogen gas directly. Certain bacteria fix nitrogen gas into ammonia or related compounds. Plants take up usable nitrogen compounds, and animals obtain nitrogen in food. Different microbes also recycle nitrogen from waste and remains, change soil nitrogen compounds, and return nitrogen gas to the air. Nitrogen helps build proteins and DNA."}], "sources": [{"title": "USGS: Water cycle", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/water-cycle", "supports": "Movement of water and its solar and gravity drivers"}, {"title": "USGS: Glossary of water cycle terms", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/glossary/glossary-water-cycle-terms", "supports": "Evaporation and condensation definitions"}, {"title": "USGS: Evapotranspiration and the water cycle", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/water-science-school/science/evapotranspiration-and-water-cycle", "supports": "Transpiration through leaves"}, {"title": "USGS: Hydrologic cycle", "url": "https://www.usgs.gov/centers/new-york-water-science-center/science/hydrologic-cycle", "supports": "Water-cycle paths and precipitation"}, {"title": "NASA: What is the carbon cycle?", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/kids/earth/what-is-the-carbon-cycle/", "supports": "Carbon movement through organisms and the environment"}, {"title": "NASA: The carbon cycle", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/the-carbon-cycle/", "supports": "Photosynthesis, respiration, and carbon"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Energy flow through ecosystems", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems", "supports": "Variable trophic transfer efficiency and energy pathways"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Themes and concepts of biology", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology", "supports": "Shared traits of life; textbooks use different groupings"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Homeostasis", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-3-homeostasis", "supports": "Internal conditions regulated within ranges"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Overview of photosynthesis", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis", "supports": "Photosynthesis inputs, outputs, and stored energy"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Using light energy to make organic molecules", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules", "supports": "Connection between photosynthesis and respiration"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Biogeochemical cycles", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-3-biogeochemical-cycles", "supports": "Microbes perform different nitrogen-cycle transformations"}, {"title": "OpenStax: Prokaryotic metabolism", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-3-prokaryotic-metabolism", "supports": "Nitrogen fixation converts atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia and usable compounds"}], "coverage": {"sourceVocabularyCount": 39, "supplementalTerms": ["Organism", "Homeostasis", "Respiration"], "questionCount": 72, "topicCounts": {"foundations": 18, "energy": 21, "relationships": 15, "cycles": 18}, "organizationLevels": ["Organism", "Population", "Community", "Ecosystem", "Biome", "Biosphere"], "lifeCharacteristicGroups": ["Cells", "Heredity/DNA", "Growth and development", "Energy use", "Response and homeostasis", "Reproduction"]}, "editorialNotes": ["The source asks for six characteristics of living things but gives no list. This review clearly labels its six-group convention, combining response and homeostasis. Individual traits are tested without asserting a universally fixed count.", "The source phrase about two predator–prey relationships is ambiguous. Content covers predation, predator–prey population effects, competition, and cooperation without inventing a canonical pair.", "The forest illustration has no supplied arrows or printed species labels. Chain questions state the feeding links. The three-consumer practice chain is a plausible hypothetical using depicted kinds of organisms, not a claim about existing arrows.", "The source symbiosis chart is partially blank; the completed relationships use +/+, +/0, and +/−, with species order interchangeable.", "Sunlight is the source for the pictured producer-based forest web. About 10% is a teaching approximation, not an exact law or a percentage of one meal.", "Respiration is qualified as aerobic when oxygen use is asserted; plants also respire. Species is presented as a useful school-level definition with limits. Reproduction applies across a species or lineage, not every individual."]};
